BOOKS_STORIES_EXPANSION["5th Grade"].push(
  {q:"What is the distinction between an author's ethos and pathos in narrative argument?",correct:"Ethos appeals to credibility and authority; pathos appeals to emotion and shared feeling — both operate simultaneously in powerful literary narrative",options:["They are the same rhetorical device","Ethos appeals to credibility and authority; pathos appeals to emotion and shared feeling — both operate simultaneously in powerful literary narrative","Only academic writing uses ethos","Pathos is always more effective"],fact:"The best literary advocacy — Uncle Tom's Cabin, In Cold Blood, The Jungle — works through pathos (emotional identification) grounded in ethos (factual credibility). Separating them weakens both!"},
  {q:"How does the relationship between form and content operate in experimental poetry?",correct:"In concrete poetry, visual arrangement is meaning; in sound poetry, phonetics are meaning — the medium stops being transparent and becomes the content itself",options:["Form is always separate from content","In concrete poetry, visual arrangement is meaning; in sound poetry, phonetics are meaning — the medium stops being transparent and becomes the content itself","Experimental poetry avoids meaning","Traditional poetry has no formal meaning"],fact:"e.e. cummings' unconventional capitalization and spacing weren't stylistic eccentricities — each formal choice contributed meaning that conventional typography couldn't express. Form and content became inseparable!"},
  {q:"What is the relationship between silence and meaning in minimalist fiction?",correct:"Hemingway's iceberg theory — omitting what you know rather than what you don't — creates resonance through absence that explicit statement cannot achieve",options:["Minimalism means shallow content","Hemingway's iceberg theory — omitting what you know rather than what you don't — creates resonance through absence that explicit statement cannot achieve","Only beginners use minimalism","Silence cannot communicate meaning in writing"],fact:"Hemingway's 'A Clean Well-Lighted Place' achieves devastating emotional power through what it refuses to explain — the waiter's existential dread is more powerful for being never explicitly named or resolved!"},
  {q:"How does the concept of 'distant reading' (Moretti) challenge traditional literary scholarship?",correct:"Analyzing patterns across thousands of texts with computational methods reveals trends invisible to close reading of individual works — changing what questions literary scholars can ask",options:["Only close reading produces valid insights","Analyzing patterns across thousands of texts with computational methods reveals patterns invisible to close reading of individual works — changing what questions literary scholars can ask","Moretti's approach is universally accepted","Distant reading replaces close reading"],fact:"Moretti's 'Graphs, Maps, Trees' used quantitative analysis to show how novel genres rise and fall — patterns impossible to see reading individual texts but obvious across hundreds or thousands!"},
  {q:"What is the significance of the global novel as a literary form in the 21st century?",correct:"Novels now written for worldwide audiences in translation navigate between local specificity and universal accessibility — creating new forms of cross-cultural literary imagining",options:["Novels are still primarily national","Novels now written for worldwide audiences in translation navigate between local specificity and universal accessibility — creating new forms of cross-cultural literary imagining","Translation makes literature less valuable","Only English novels have global reach"],fact:"Elena Ferrante, Karl Ove Knausgård, and Haruki Murakami write intensely local stories that simultaneously achieve massive worldwide readership — their very particularity creates the universal resonance that generic global fiction cannot!"}
);

