FLAGS_COUNTRIES_EXPANSION["5th Grade"].push(
  {q:"What is the concept of 'extraterritoriality' and how does it affect sovereignty?",correct:"When a country's laws apply to its citizens abroad, or when foreign nationals on a country's territory are subject to their home country's jurisdiction, it complicates sovereign authority",options:["Sovereignty ends exactly at borders","When a country's laws apply to its citizens abroad, or when foreign nationals on a country's territory are subject to their home country's jurisdiction, it complicates sovereign authority","Extraterritoriality only applies to diplomats","It's only relevant for maritime law"],fact:"US FACTA law requires foreign banks to report American account holders — US tax law extraterritorially imposed on Swiss banks that had nothing to do with the US, but complied to maintain dollar access!"},
  {q:"What is the significance of the Arctic Council for great power competition?",correct:"As the Arctic opens due to climate change, the Arctic Council's eight members (including Russia and the US) must govern increasing competition for resources and shipping routes",options:["The Arctic Council has no strategic significance","As the Arctic opens due to climate change, the Arctic Council's eight members (including Russia and the US) must govern increasing competition for resources and shipping routes","Only Russia has Arctic interests","The Arctic Council has a military force"],fact:"Russia's dramatic 2007 submarine planting of a flag on the Arctic seabed at the North Pole was a direct political act asserting territorial claims — theatrically demonstrating how the Arctic is becoming contested geopolitical terrain!"},
  {q:"How does the law of the sea (UNCLOS) create a framework for flag state jurisdiction?",correct:"Ships fly a national flag indicating which country's laws govern them at sea — 'flags of convenience' allow ship owners to register in countries with favorable regulations",options:["Ships have no national identity","Ships fly a national flag indicating which country's laws govern them at sea — 'flags of convenience' allow ship owners to register in countries with favorable regulations","All ships use the same international rules","Flag state jurisdiction is being eliminated"],fact:"Panama and Liberia are the world's largest ship registries despite having minimal actual shipping industries — ship owners choose them for low taxes and lax regulations, undermining global labor and environmental standards!"},
  {q:"What is the significance of 'strategic depth' as a geographic security concept?",correct:"Countries with large territories can trade space for time — retreating while an invader overextends, as Russia did against Napoleon and Hitler",options:["Size is irrelevant to security","Countries with large territories can trade space for time — retreating while an invader overextends, as Russia did against Napoleon and Hitler","Small countries are always more vulnerable","Strategic depth only applies to naval warfare"],fact:"Israel has no strategic depth — it's only 9 miles wide at its narrowest point. This geographic vulnerability directly shapes Israeli military doctrine toward preemptive strikes rather than defensive strategies!"},
  {q:"What is the geopolitical significance of rare earth minerals for the world map?",correct:"China controls about 60% of rare earth mining and 85% of processing — creating supply chain dependencies that give it significant geopolitical leverage over high-tech manufacturing nations",options:["Rare earths are evenly distributed globally","China controls about 60% of rare earth mining and 85% of processing — creating supply chain dependencies that give it significant geopolitical leverage over high-tech manufacturing nations","Rare earths are only relevant for electronics","The US controls rare earth supplies"],fact:"When China restricted rare earth exports to Japan during a 2010 territorial dispute, it demonstrated how resource geography creates coercive leverage — Japan quickly developed alternative sources, recognizing the vulnerability!"},
  {q:"What is the significance of the Treaty of Westphalia for flag symbolism?",correct:"By establishing sovereign nation-states as the primary political units, Westphalia made national flags the authoritative symbols of political belonging — each flag representing an independent sovereign entity",options:["Flag symbolism predates nation-states","By establishing sovereign nation-states as the primary political units, Westphalia made national flags the authoritative symbols of political belonging — each flag representing an independent sovereign entity","Flags existed before Westphalia with the same meaning","Only the UN defines flag significance"],fact:"Before Westphalia, people's primary political loyalty was to the Catholic Church, feudal lords, or city-states — Westphalian sovereignty created the nation-state system that made national flags meaningful symbols of identity!"},
  {q:"How does 'middle power' theory explain the diplomatic strategies of countries like Canada, Australia, and Norway?",correct:"Middle powers use multilateral institutions, international law, and coalition-building to punch above their weight in global affairs — substituting diplomatic creativity for military might",options:["Middle powers have no significant role","Middle powers use multilateral institutions, international law, and coalition-building to punch above their weight in global affairs — substituting diplomatic creativity for military might","Middle powers simply follow great powers","Military power alone determines international influence"],fact:"Canada's development of UN peacekeeping, Australia's role in founding the Chemical Weapons Convention, Norway's Oslo Accords facilitation — all examples of middle powers shaping global outcomes disproportionate to their size!"},
  {q:"What is the significance of the contested status of Jerusalem for understanding intractable territorial disputes?",correct:"Jerusalem's conflicting claims by Israel, Palestine, and religious communities globally illustrates how identity, religion, history, and security interests can make territorial resolution nearly impossible",options:["Jerusalem's status is nearly resolved","Jerusalem's conflicting claims by Israel, Palestine, and religious communities globally illustrates how identity, religion, history, and security interests can make territorial resolution nearly impossible","The UN has resolved Jerusalem's status","Religious sites can always be managed diplomatically"],fact:"Jerusalem contains sites sacred to Judaism, Christianity, and Islam simultaneously — the Western Wall, Church of the Holy Sepulchre, and Dome of the Rock all within walking distance, making any territorial division politically explosive!"},
  {q:"What is the concept of 'normative power' and how does the EU exercise it globally?",correct:"The EU projects power primarily by making access to its market conditional on adopting its standards — spreading rule of law, human rights, and environmental regulations through economic incentives",options:["The EU only uses military power","The EU projects power primarily by making access to its market conditional on adopting its standards — spreading rule of law, human rights, and environmental regulations through economic incentives","Normative power is less effective than military power","Only authoritarian states use conditionality"],fact:"The 'Brussels Effect' — where EU regulations become de facto global standards because companies find it easier to meet one standard — means the EU shapes global trade rules without any military coercion whatsoever!"}
);

