POKEMON_EXPANSION["4th Grade"].push(
  {q:"What is the competitive significance of 'base stat totals' (BST) in Pokémon?",correct:"BST measures a Pokémon's overall stat total, but distribution matters more — a Pokémon with 600 BST concentrated in one stat often beats one with evenly spread 600 BST",options:["Higher BST always means better Pokémon","BST measures a Pokémon's overall stat total, but distribution matters more — a Pokémon with 600 BST concentrated in one stat often beats one with evenly spread 600 BST","BST determines tier placement directly","Legendary Pokémon always have highest BST"],fact:"Smeargle has pathetically low BST (250) but is competitively used at high levels — its Sketch ability letting it learn any move makes it a premier lead despite being statistically 'weak.' Utility transcends raw numbers!"},
  {q:"What is the significance of the 'stall' playstyle in competitive Pokémon?",correct:"Stall teams use defensive cores, recovery, hazards, and status conditions to win by outlasting opponents rather than defeating them through offensive damage",options:["Stall is not competitive viable","Stall teams use defensive cores, recovery, hazards, and status conditions to win by outlasting opponents rather than defeating them through offensive damage","Stall only works at low levels","Only Blissey is used in stall teams"],fact:"Stall vs. Hyperoffense is one of competitive Pokémon's great stylistic tensions — players and analysts debate whether offensive pressure or defensive attrition is the more fundamental winning strategy. Elite players have won majors with both!"},
  {q:"What is the competitive significance of 'setup sweepers' in Pokémon battles?",correct:"Pokémon that use one or two turns boosting stats (Swords Dance, Dragon Dance, Nasty Plot) then sweep through entire teams are central to many team architectures",options:["Setup moves waste turns","Pokémon that use one or two turns boosting stats (Swords Dance, Dragon Dance, Nasty Plot) then sweep through entire teams are central to many team architectures","Setup sweepers are never used defensively","Setup moves are banned in competitive play"],fact:"A +2 Swords Dance Garchomp is almost impossible to stop without the exact right resistance or revenge killer — creating the strategic question of whether opponents can afford to let Garchomp set up or must sacrifice a Pokémon to interrupt!"},
  {q:"What are the implications of 'Trick Room' as a win condition in competitive Pokémon?",correct:"Trick Room reverses Speed priority for 5 turns, making the slowest Pokémon move first — enabling an entirely different team archetype built around maximally slow, powerful Pokémon",options:["Trick Room is a minor strategy","Trick Room reverses Speed priority for 5 turns, making the slowest Pokémon move first — enabling an entirely different team archetype built around maximally slow, powerful Pokémon","Trick Room only helps one Pokémon","Trick Room is never used at high levels"],fact:"Trick Room teams featuring 0 Speed IVs (deliberately making Pokémon as slow as possible!) represent one of competitive Pokémon's most counterintuitive and conceptually elegant strategies — turning the game's usual logic completely upside down!"},
  {q:"What is the significance of how Pokémon abilities interact with weather conditions?",correct:"Drizzle (permanent rain), Drought (sun), Sand Stream (sand), and Snow Warning (hail/snow) create weather on entry, enabling ability-weather-move synergy chains that form entire competitive archetypes",options:["Weather abilities are minor in competitive play","Drizzle (permanent rain), Drought (sun), Sand Stream (sand), and Snow Warning (hail/snow) create weather on entry, enabling ability-weather-move synergy chains that form entire competitive archetypes","Weather only affects appearance","Weather abilities were removed in Gen VI"],fact:"Swift Swim doubles Speed in rain + Drizzle Politoed setting rain automatically + Water STAB in rain = rain sweep archetype. These layered synergies reward deep mechanical knowledge and creative team building!"},
  {q:"What makes the competitive use of status conditions strategically complex?",correct:"Status conditions (burn halving Attack, paralysis reducing Speed, poison providing chip) create win conditions through indirect damage while requiring prediction and timing to apply effectively",options:["Status conditions are rarely used competitively","Status conditions (burn halving Attack, paralysis reducing Speed, poison providing chip) create win conditions through indirect damage while requiring prediction and timing to apply effectively","Only poison is competitively relevant","Status moves are too slow to use"],fact:"Will-O-Wisp on physical attackers is one of competitive Pokémon's most elegant strategies — a single move that completely neutralizes physical sweepers by permanently halving their Attack stat for the rest of the battle!"},
  {q:"What is the difference between 'wall' and 'tank' Pokémon in competitive team building?",correct:"Walls are defensive Pokémon that take hits without KO-ing opponents; tanks can take hits AND threaten damage — tanks are generally more valuable for applying offensive pressure",options:["Walls and tanks are identical roles","Walls are defensive Pokémon that take hits without KO-ing opponents; tanks can take hits AND threaten damage — tanks are generally more valuable for applying offensive pressure","Pure defense is always the strongest approach","Only one defensive Pokémon is needed per team"],fact:"Toxapex is a wall — pure defense with minimal offensive presence. Landorus-T is a tank — taking hits while threatening massive damage. Teams need the right balance of purely defensive anchors and offensive flexibility!"},
  {q:"What is the competitive significance of Pokémon 'revenge killing' and 'cleaning' roles?",correct:"Revenge killers defeat one weakened threat; cleaners sweep through multiple weakened opponents late-game — both require Speed and KO power but serve distinct strategic functions",options:["Revenge killing and cleaning are identical","Revenge killers defeat one weakened threat; cleaners sweep through multiple weakened opponents late-game — both require Speed and KO power but serve distinct strategic functions","Cleaning roles aren't competitive viable","Revenge killing only occurs in late game"],fact:"A Choice Scarf Garchomp as revenge killer vs. a Dragon Dance Dragonite as cleaner represent these different roles perfectly — one activates immediately to neutralize threats; the other sets up once threats are removed to sweep remaining opponents!"}
);

POKEMON_EXPANSION["5th Grade"].push(
  {q:"What is the difference between 'matchup fishing' and 'team building for consistency' in competitive Pokémon?",correct:"Matchup fishing brings hyper-specific teams hoping to face favorable opponents; consistent teams handle multiple threats regardless of opposing team — competitive philosophy divide",options:["All competitive teams seek consistency","Matchup fishing brings hyper-specific teams hoping to face favorable opponents; consistent teams handle multiple threats regardless of opposing team — competitive philosophy divide","Consistency and matchup fishing are the same","Only matchup fishing teams win tournaments"],fact:"Swiss rounds reward consistency (playing many opponents) while single-elimination finals reward matchup fishing (one bad draw ends everything) — the same format shift changes which team-building philosophy is optimal!"},
  {q:"How does the Pokémon Trading Card Game mechanic design differ philosophically from the video game?",correct:"The TCG uses resource management (Energy cards), hand advantage, and deck-building as primary strategic variables — completely different from video game's stat-based turn-by-turn calculation",options:["TCG and video game use identical strategy","The TCG uses resource management (Energy cards), hand advantage, and deck-building as primary strategic variables — completely different from video game's stat-based turn-by-turn calculation","The TCG is simpler than the video game","Only collector value distinguishes them"],fact:"Expert video game players and expert TCG players require completely different skill sets — a video game champion can be a terrible TCG player because the games share theme but not strategic depth!"},
  {q:"What is the significance of data mining in competitive Pokémon communities?",correct:"Players extract unreleased content from game code to prepare teams and strategies before official release — giving early adopters significant competitive advantage",options:["Data mining is considered cheating","Players extract unreleased content from game code to prepare teams and strategies before official release — giving early adopters significant competitive advantage","Game Freak embraces data mining","Data mining only reveals cosmetic content"],fact:"When Pokémon Scarlet/Violet launched, data miners had complete move sets, abilities, and base stats within 24 hours — competitive players used this information to prepare tournament teams while others were still discovering the games normally!"},
  {q:"How does the psychological dimension of team preview change competitive Pokémon strategy?",correct:"Seeing all 6 opponent Pokémon before battle begins transforms the game from pure tactical to strategic — lead choice, switch prediction, and win condition identification all happen before turn 1",options:["Team preview has no strategic value","Seeing all 6 opponent Pokémon before battle begins transforms the game from pure tactical to strategic — lead choice, switch prediction, and win condition identification all happen before turn 1","Team preview was always in the games","Team preview only matters in doubles"],fact:"Experienced players can often identify the opponent's full strategy from team preview alone — recognizing standard cores, predicting which Pokémon will be leads, and selecting the optimal counter-strategy before the battle even begins!"},
  {q:"What is the significance of 'speed control' as a metagame concept in competitive Pokémon?",correct:"Controlling which Pokémon move first through Trick Room, Tailwind, speed-boosting moves, or Choice Scarf is often the most important meta-level strategic element in team composition",options:["Speed is the only important stat","Controlling which Pokémon move first through Trick Room, Tailwind, speed-boosting moves, or Choice Scarf is often the most important meta-level strategic element in team composition","Speed control only matters in singles","Only base Speed stats determine move order"],fact:"A tournament victory can be decided by a single Speed tie before any moves are chosen — the rock-paper-scissors of speed control (Trick Room vs. Tailwind vs. Choice Scarf) shapes entire metagame archetypes at the team-building stage!"},
  {q:"What is the relationship between Pokémon lore and real-world cultural mythology across different regional games?",correct:"Each generation's mythological framework draws from different real cultures — Unova uses American mythology, Kalos French culture, Alola Hawaiian tradition, Galar British folklore",options:["All Pokémon games use Japanese mythology","Each generation's mythological framework draws from different real cultures — Unova uses American mythology, Kalos French culture, Alola Hawaiian tradition, Galar British folklore","Regional lore is purely cosmetic","Mythology only affects legendary Pokémon"],fact:"Alola's Tapu guardians draw from Hawaiian akua (divine entities) and kapu (sacred restrictions) — Game Freak researched Hawaiian culture to create a regionally specific mythology. The deepening cultural specificity of each region shows increasing storytelling ambition!"},
  {q:"How does the history of competitive Pokémon mirror the development of other esports in terms of community infrastructure?",correct:"Like other esports, Pokémon competition evolved from informal gatherings to structured circuits with streaming, sponsorship, and career infrastructure over 20+ years",options:["Pokémon competition has no professional infrastructure","Like other esports, Pokémon competition evolved from informal gatherings to structured circuits with streaming, sponsorship, and career infrastructure over 20+ years","Pokémon esports is entirely corporate","The Pokémon competitive community remains purely amateur"],fact:"The Pokémon World Championship began as small regional events in 2004 and now draws thousands of participants, millions of online viewers, and has produced players who have competed for over a decade across multiple generations of games!"},
  {q:"What is the philosophical debate between 'fun' and 'optimal play' in casual versus competitive Pokémon?",correct:"Most players engage with Pokémon casually while competitive players optimize — but competitive optimization can drain the joy of exploration and discovery that defines casual play",options:["There is no debate — optimal is always better","Most players engage with Pokémon casually while competitive players optimize — but competitive optimization can drain the joy of exploration and discovery that defines casual play","Casual play is not legitimate","Only competitive play represents the 'true' game"],fact:"Game Freak deliberately keeps the main games completable with any team — protecting the casual experience — while hiding competitive mechanics for those who seek them. The same game serving both audiences requires deliberate design restraint!"},
  {q:"How does Pokémon's approach to gender mechanics across generations reflect evolving cultural awareness?",correct:"Early games used binary gender mechanics tied to stats; later games decoupled gender from competitive optimization and introduced non-binary character options reflecting broader social evolution",options:["Gender mechanics have never changed","Early games used binary gender mechanics tied to stats; later games decoupled gender from competitive optimization and introduced non-binary character options reflecting broader social evolution","Gender has no function in Pokémon games","Player character gender has always been optional"],fact:"The hairstyle options in Gen IX allowed all options for all characters regardless of gender selection — a quiet but meaningful design change acknowledging that clothing and appearance aren't inherently gendered even within a children's franchise!"},
  {q:"What is the significance of fan-generated competitive content (Showdown, Smogon, damage calculators) for understanding distributed cultural production?",correct:"The Pokémon competitive community has built infrastructure exceeding what Nintendo/Game Freak officially created — demonstrating how passionate communities extend and sustain cultural products beyond corporate control",options:["Fan content depends entirely on official support","The Pokémon competitive community has built infrastructure exceeding what Nintendo/Game Freak officially created — demonstrating how passionate communities extend and sustain cultural products beyond corporate control","Fan-created tools are less reliable than official ones","Nintendo controls all Pokémon competitive content"],fact:"Pokémon Showdown has millions of battles per day; Smogon's strategy guides are more comprehensive than any official resource; community damage calculators are more accurate tools for battle preparation than any Nintendo-provided resource. Fans built the competitive game's entire foundation!"}
);

